  "body": {
    "id": "prop:no-breakout-length",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\theta\\in\\mathcal N_\\rho\\), any random interval with coverage at least \\(1-\\alpha_0\\) at both \\(\\theta\\) and \\(S\\theta\\) satisfies \\(\\mathbb E_\\theta[\\operatorname{length}(\\mathcal C_n)]\\ge(1-2\\alpha_0)\\lvert\\gamma/\\alpha\\rvert\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\theta",
      "\\mathcal N_\\rho",
      "\\alpha_0",
      "S",
      "\\mathcal C_n",
      "\\gamma",
      "\\alpha"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:confounding-bound",
      "def:no-breakout-branch",
      "thm:identification-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "Exact observational equivalence forces a nonvanishing honest interval at the no-breakout boundary.",
    "gap": "This honest-interval consequence is an incremental application of the standard two-point coverage argument; Kivva et al.'s population nonidentification result does not state the \\(1-2\\alpha_0\\) expected-length factor.",
    "consumer": "The proposition prevents a narrow reported interval from masking exact source-label ambiguity when every flag vanishes.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(\\theta\\in\\mathcal N_\\rho\\) and put \\(q=\\gamma/\\alpha\\).  The treatment-loading lower bound ensures \\(\\alpha\\neq0\\), and the identification-frontier theorem gives\n\\[\n P_\\theta=P_{S\\theta},\\qquad\n \\beta(S\\theta)=\\beta(\\theta)+q.                     \\tag{62}\n\\]\nLet \\(I_n\\) be any random interval having coverage at least \\(1-\\alpha_0\\) at both structural parameters.  Under the common distribution in (62), define\n\\[\n A_0=\\{\\beta(\\theta)\\in I_n\\},\\qquad\n A_1=\\{\\beta(\\theta)+q\\in I_n\\}.\n\\]\nThe two coverage inequalities and the union bound give\n\\[\n \\mathbb P_\\theta(A_0\\cap A_1)\n \\geq1-\\mathbb P_\\theta(A_0^c)-\\mathbb P_\\theta(A_1^c)\n \\geq1-2\\alpha_0.                                   \\tag{63}\n\\]\nOn \\(A_0\\cap A_1\\), an interval contains two points separated by \\(|q|\\), so its length is at least \\(|q|\\).  Since length is nonnegative, (63) yields\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_\\theta[\\operatorname{length}(I_n)]\n \\geq(1-2\\alpha_0)|q|\n =(1-2\\alpha_0)|\\gamma/\\alpha|,\n\\]\nwhich is the asserted bound.  The condition \\(\\alpha_0<1/2\\) makes its coefficient positive.",
